<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russian Orthodox Church</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> is the branch of Eastern Orthodox Christianity that developed after the conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a> in the late tenth century. It became closely tied to Muscovite and then Russian imperial authority, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> placed it under state control by replacing the patriarchate with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-synod">Holy Synod</a> in 1721. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> treat Orthodoxy as a central pillar of tsarist legitimacy and identity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church.jpg" alt="Russian Orthodox Church" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Crawshaw · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Basils_Cathedral-500px.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The history of the church begins with the Christianization of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, which commenced in 988 with the baptism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a> and his subjects by clergy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecumenical-patriarch-of-constantinople">Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople</a>. Christianity had already begun to take root in the 10th century: Christians are mentioned among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> towards the end of the reign of Igor, and his wife Olga was baptized in the mid-10th century, though scholars dispute the year, between 946 and 960, and the place. Her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> opposed conversion. A later tradition, found in the 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, holds that the Apostle Andrew visited the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, although there is no historical evidence for it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Mongol invasions <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> lost its significance, and Metropolitan Maximus moved his seat to Vladimir in 1299. His successor Peter moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1325, allied himself with the prince of Moscow and was buried in the Dormition Cathedral, so designating Moscow as the future centre of the church. The church declared autocephaly in 1448 by electing its own metropolitan without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>'s blessing, and in 1589 the metropolitan see became a patriarchate with Constantinople's consent. After the fall of Constantinople the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philotheus-of-pskov">Philotheus of Pskov</a> formulated the idea of Moscow as the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p>In the mid-17th century liturgical reforms led to the Schism of the Russian Church, and its opponents formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-believers">Old Believers</a>. After Patriarch Adrian died in 1700, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> chose not to have a successor elected. In 1721 he replaced the patriarch with the Most Holy Synod, made up of appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, monks and priests and overseen by an ober-procurator who reported to the emperor. The Synodal period lasted until 1917.</p><p>In 1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had 55,173 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches, 550 monasteries and 475 convents. A Local Council opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in August 1917 and restored the patriarchate, and on 5 November Metropolitan Tikhon of Moscow was chosen as the first patriarch in about 200 years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> treated the clergy and active believers as anti-revolutionary elements, who were prosecuted, arrested, exiled and imprisoned, and the church was heavily persecuted. Religious belief nevertheless persisted. Under Patriarch Aleksey II, who took office in 1990, some 15,000 churches were reopened or built.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The church is hierarchical. A parish, usually a single church building and its attendees, is headed by a priest; the parishes of a region belong to an eparchy, equivalent to a Western diocese, governed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Its autonomous branches include the Japanese Orthodox Church and the Chinese Orthodox Church. The Russian Orthodox Church Outside of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, established by émigrés in the 1920s, reconciled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 2007 as a self-governing body.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Russian Orthodox Church” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He reorganised the army, built a navy almost from nothing, and subordinated the <b>Russian Orthodox Church</b> to the state by replacing the Patriarchate with the Holy Synod in 1721.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Vladimir Putin, Russia has promoted explicitly conservative policies at home and abroad, in close collaboration with the <b>Russian Orthodox Church</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Russian Orthodox Church</b> remains in Alaska.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-america">Russian America</a></li></ul></section>
